---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Base title="Privacy policy" description="How Open World Life handles your location and data: it all stays on your phone.">
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: {site.policiesUpdated}</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>In short:</strong> your location and your progress stay on your phone. There are no accounts, no analytics,
      no ads and no tracking, and we never receive a record of where you’ve been.
    </div>

    <p>
      {site.name} (“the app”) is a game that clears a fog from the map as you explore the real world. This policy
      explains what the app does with information, and is published by {site.publisher} (“we”).
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your location.</strong> The app uses your phone’s location to work out which parts of the map you’ve
        visited. Location is processed on your phone only. It is never sent to us, and we keep no record of where you have
        been.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your progress.</strong> The areas you’ve unlocked, the regions you’ve visited and your settings are stored
        on your phone.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If your phone’s own backup is turned on (iCloud on iPhone, Google backup on Android), your progress is included in
      that backup so it can be restored on a new phone. Those backups are handled by Apple or Google under their privacy
      terms; we cannot access them.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app downloads</h2>
    <p>
      To show the map and the regions you explore, the app downloads public map data from the services below. As with any
      website, they receive your device’s IP address and which part of the map is being requested, which is needed to
      send the right data. They do not receive your location history, and we send them no information about you.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenFreeMap</strong> (tiles.openfreemap.org): map images and map data, including lakes and rivers. See{' '}
        <a href="https://openfreemap.org">openfreemap.org</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Our content delivery network</strong> (Amazon CloudFront, operated for us by Amazon Web Services): region
        boundaries and the map style. Like any web server it keeps standard request logs for operating the service; we do
        not use them to identify or track anyone.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors.</p>

    <h2>What we don’t do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No accounts, sign-in or profiles.</li>
      <li>No analytics, advertising, tracking or data brokers.</li>
      <li>No selling or sharing of personal information.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sharing your progress</h2>
    <p>
      If you choose to share your progress, the app makes an image with a region’s outline, its name and your percentage,
      and hands it to your phone’s share sheet. It never includes the streets you’ve explored. Where it goes after that is
      up to you.
    </p>

    <h2>Permissions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Location while using the app:</strong> to clear the fog where you are.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Location always (optional):</strong> to keep clearing the fog while your phone is in your pocket. Turn it
        off any time in the app or in your phone’s settings.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Everything is on your phone. Use <strong>Settings → Reset all progress</strong> in the app, or delete the app, to
      remove it. Remove old phone backups through iCloud or Google if you want those gone too.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>The app does not collect personal information from anyone, including children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, we’ll update this page and the date above.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>{site.publisher}, <a href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}>{site.supportEmail}</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
